        "problem": "$[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]=$",
        "worked_solution": "Here, the first factor is $4\\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n4\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}\u2713\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 3=4\\times 3\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot 1 & 4\\cdot 0 & 4\\cdot 4 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 0 & 2\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0 & 1\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0 & 1\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
